The proposed answer is incorrect on the central fact. While the background details are largely accurate—the successful 1866 expedition did sail from Valentia, Ireland, landing the cable at Heart's Content, Newfoundland on July 27, 1866, under Cyrus Field's auspices, and it was the only ship large enough to carry the entire cable—the ship itself was not the Great Western.

The correct ship was the SS Great Eastern, Isambard Kingdom Brunel's massive iron steamship, which was the largest ship in the world at the time and the only vessel capable of carrying the full length of cable in one load. The Great Eastern successfully completed the 1866 expedition and even returned to recover and complete the lost 1865 cable afterward. The Great Western (another Brunel ship) was famous for early transatlantic passenger service but was not the cable-laying vessel of 1866.

