The proposed answer names Émile Nouguier, but the historical record attributes the first sketch to **Maurice Koechlin**. In May 1884, Koechlin, a senior engineer at Eiffel & Cie, made the first rough sketch of the tower at his home. He then worked with Émile Nouguier (the other principal engineer) to develop the concept, and architect Stephen Sauvestre added the decorative embellishments. Eiffel later acquired the patent rights and promoted the design.

The proposed answer correctly describes the context and even the collaborative development, but assigns the sketch to the wrong engineer — Nouguier was Koechlin's collaborator, not the one who drew the initial sketch.

