The proposed answer is incorrect. Joan Martorell was indeed an architect associated with the Sagrada Família story — he was the diocesan architect who recommended Gaudí as his own successor — but he did not design the crypt. The original architect, commissioned by Josep Maria Bocabella's Spiritual Association of Devotees of St. Joseph, was **Francisco de Paula del Villar y Lozano**, who designed the neo-Gothic church, oversaw the laying of the foundation stone in March 1882, and constructed the crypt before resigning in late 1883 following disagreements with the promoter (over the cost of materials, per some accounts). Martorell was then offered the position, declined it, and proposed his assistant Gaudí, who took over in late 1883.

The proposed answer has the right narrative structure (crypt, 1882 start, 1883 resignation) but attributes it to the wrong person.

