The OECD Convention was signed in Paris on 14 December 1960 and entered into force on 30 September 1961. The OEEC had 18 European members by the late 1950s (including later additions such as West Germany and Spain), and the OECD was created by adding the United States and Canada to that group. That yields 20 founding member countries: Austria, Belgium, Canada, Denmark, France, West Germany, Greece, Iceland, Ireland, Italy, Luxembourg, Netherlands, Norway, Portugal, Spain, Sweden, Switzerland, Turkey, United Kingdom, and United States.

The proposed answer correctly identifies the 18 European OEEC members but then fails to add the U.S. and Canada properly, undercounting the founding total. The founding membership at entry into force was 20, not 18.

