The proposed answer is incorrect. When Alfred Marshall retired in 1908, he was succeeded as Professor of Political Economy at Cambridge by Arthur Cecil Pigou, who had been Marshall's student and colleague. Pigou held the chair from 1908 until 1943 and was known for his work on welfare economics (the concept of "Pigouvian taxes" bears his name).

John Maynard Keynes never held the Chair of Political Economy at Cambridge. In 1908, Keynes was indeed a young Cambridge figure, but he was working in the India Office (returning to Cambridge as a Fellow of King's College that year), not as a professor. Keynes famously disagreed with Pigou's approach to economics and later criticized Pigou in *The General Theory*.

